          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.087,
            0.848,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "The squares of natural numbers are called **perfect squares.** For example, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, … are all perfect squares."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.144,
            0.68,
            0.167
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Patterns and Properties of Perfect Squares"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.175,
            0.847,
            0.197
          ],
          "content": "Find the squares of the first 30 natural numbers and fill in the table below."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.252,
            0.199,
            0.772,
            0.512
          ],
          "content": "| 1<sup>2</sup> = 1 | 11<sup>2</sup> = 121 | 21<sup>2</sup> = 441 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 2<sup>2</sup> = 4 | 12<sup>2</sup> = | 22<sup>2</sup>= |\n| 3<sup>2</sup> = 9 | 13<sup>2</sup> = |  |\n| 4<sup>2</sup> = 16 | 14<sup>2</sup> = |  |\n| 5<sup>2</sup> = 25 | 15<sup>2</sup> = |  |\n| 6<sup>2</sup> = | 16<sup>2</sup> = |  |\n| 7<sup>2</sup> = | 17<sup>2</sup> = |  |\n| 8<sup>2</sup> = | 18<sup>2</sup> = |  |\n| 9<sup>2</sup> = | 19<sup>2</sup> = |  |\n| 10<sup>2</sup> = | 20<sup>2</sup> = |  |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_table_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.518,
            0.801,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": "What patterns do you notice? Share your observations and make conjectures."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.816,
            0.508,
            0.904,
            0.564
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.562,
            0.847,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": "Study the squares in the table above. What are the digits in the units places of these numbers? All these numbers end with 0, 1, 4, 5, 6 or 9. None of them end with 2, 3, 7 or 8."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.621,
            0.712,
            0.644
          ],
          "content": "If a number ends in 0, 1, 4, 5, 6 or 9, is it always a square?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.817,
            0.601,
            0.904,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.646,
            0.849,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "The numbers 16 and 36 are both squares with 6 in the units place. 1 However, 26, whose units digit is also 6, is not a square. Therefore, we cannot determine if a number is a square just by looking at the digit in the units place. But, the units digit can tell us when a number is not a square. If a number ends with 2, 3, 7, or 8, then we can definitely say that it is not a square."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.76,
            0.848,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": "？ Write 5 numbers such that you can determine by looking at their units digit that they are not squares."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.802,
            0.848,
            0.861
          ],
          "content": "The squares, $1 ^ { 2 } , 9 ^ { 2 } , 1 1 ^ { 2 } , 1 9 ^ { 2 } , 2 1 ^ { 2 }$ and $2 9 ^ { 2 } ;$ all have 1 in their units place. Write the next two squares. Notice that if a number has 1 or 9 in the units place, then its square ends in 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.862,
            0.846,
            0.902
          ],
          "content": "？ Let us consider square numbers ending in 6: $1 6 = 4 ^ { 2 } ,$ $3 6 \\: = \\: 6 ^ { 2 }$ $1 9 6 = 1 4 ^ { 2 } ,$ $256 = 16^{2}, 576 = 24^{2}$ , and $6 7 6 = 2 6 ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
